# Implementation Plan: OpenCode Integration into AI Assistant Panel

Integrate **OpenCode** — an open-source, model-agnostic AI coding agent ([opencode.ai](https://opencode.ai)) — as a selectable **Agent Mode** inside the existing AI Assistant Panel in Local Study Notebook. This is a deliberate part of the app's vision: the assistant should be able to help with coding and research tasks, not just study-note chat. OpenCode natively supports 75+ LLM providers and exposes a documented HTTP API, making its provider-routing layer reusable here without hand-maintaining vendor quirks ourselves.

**Revision note:** this supersedes the original draft. Six corrections were made after fact-checking OpenCode's actual docs and reviewing the draft for security/integrity gaps — each is called out inline below with a "Corrected:" marker.

---

## Background: What is OpenCode?

OpenCode is a terminal-native AI coding agent designed for agentic, multi-step tasks:
- **Model-agnostic**: Works with OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, Ollama (Hermes), and 70+ others — its provider-adapter layer is the thing we want to borrow, so the routing bugs fixed this session (Gemini endpoint shape, `thought_signature`, model deprecation) don't need to be re-litigated for this second assistant mode.
- **BYOK**: Full "Bring Your Own Key" support.
- **Interfaces**: Terminal TUI, desktop app, IDE extension, and a documented **HTTP server API** (`opencode serve`).
- **Agentic tools**: File editing, code search, git operations, LSP integration, shell execution.
- **Installation**: standalone binaries for Windows/macOS/Linux — **no Node.js required** (see below).

---

## Relationship to the existing "Study Assistant" tab

This is an **additive, opt-in mode**, not a replacement:
- **💬 Study Assistant** (existing, hardened this session) stays the default — plain BYOK chat, read-only workspace tools, no shell/git access. Lowest risk, always available.
- **🤖 OpenCode Agent** (new) — a separate, explicitly-entered mode with materially more capability (file editing, shell, git) and its own security posture. Keeping these as distinct tabs, not a single merged chat, is the right shape precisely because the two modes have very different risk profiles.

