## Corrected: Installation Strategy (no Node.js dependency)

The original draft assumed `npm install -g opencode-ai`, requiring a Node.js runtime most students won't have. Confirmed OpenCode ships standalone binaries:

- **Windows**: `winget install OpenCode.OpenCode`, or the signed MSI, or a portable binary release — none require Node.js.
- **macOS/Linux**: `curl -fsSL https://opencode.ai/install | bash` — downloads a standalone binary, no Node.js required either.

`check_opencode_installed()` should run `opencode --version` regardless of *how* it was installed — detection doesn't change, only the install-guidance copy shown to the user does (point at winget/MSI on Windows, the curl script elsewhere, not `npm install -g`).

---

## Corrected: Embed Mechanism (no iframe — `serve` is a headless API, not a UI)

Fact-checked against OpenCode's own docs: `opencode serve` exposes a **headless OpenAPI 3.1 HTTP server** (spec viewable at `/doc`) — there is no web UI to point an `<iframe>` at. The original draft's "spawn `opencode serve`, embed in an iframe" step doesn't work as written.

**Chosen approach: build a custom chat/agent UI in React against OpenCode's documented HTTP API.**
- Pros: stays visually consistent with the rest of the app, avoids embedding a third-party web app's DOM/JS inside the Tauri webview (smaller security surface than an iframe of code we don't control), full control over how tool-call activity (file edits, shell commands, git ops) is surfaced to the user for approval/visibility.
- Cons: more UI implementation work than an iframe would have been — `OpenCodeView.tsx` now needs to actually implement a streaming chat view against the API, not just host a frame.

**Rejected alternative:** using OpenCode's own separate browser-launch feature (opens a system browser tab on a random port). This works but breaks the "stays inside the app panel" goal, so it's not the primary path — worth keeping as a manual fallback link ("Open in browser instead") if the embedded API client has problems.

