## Corrected: Vault Integrity Reconciliation (reuse existing infrastructure)

The original draft didn't address what happens when OpenCode's file-editing/shell tools modify vault files directly, bypassing this app's own save pipeline (`write_local_file`), local version history, and vault index.

**Fix: reuse infrastructure that already exists in this codebase** (per recent commits — vault index sync and local version history are already implemented):
- Subscribe to the `/event` SSE stream (see Spike Results below) and invoke the existing `reindex_vault` command reactively whenever a `part.updated` event indicates a file-editing tool ran — no polling or guessing when a session ended.
- Whether agent edits also get their own version-history entries is a separate product decision — see Spike Results item 3.

---

## Corrected: Local Server Authentication

The original draft didn't mention securing the spawned local server at all. OpenCode's server supports `OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD` (HTTP basic auth, username defaults to `opencode`) and CORS configuration.

- Generate a random password per session when spawning the sidecar; pass it via `OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD`.
- Hold that credential only in memory in the frontend (Zustand state, not persisted to `localStorage` or written to disk) for the lifetime of the session.
- Never spawn the server without a password — an unauthenticated local HTTP endpoint with file/shell/git tool access is a meaningfully worse local attack surface than anything in the Study Assistant tab.

---

## Corrected: Process Lifecycle via Tauri's Sidecar API

The original draft's `spawn_opencode_server` / `kill_opencode_server` commands implied hand-rolled PID tracking. `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json` currently has no shell/sidecar plugin permission at all (only `core:default`, `dialog:*`, `updater:default`, `process:default`).

- Add `tauri-plugin-shell` as a dependency and register its `sidecar` capability in `default.json` (per this project's own CLAUDE.md rule: new plugins need a capability entry).
- Use the plugin's sidecar API for spawn/kill instead of raw process handling — this gets auto-kill-on-app-exit and more predictable crash/lifecycle behavior for free, rather than reimplementing it.

